### Operational Experience: IETF NOC Agent (Joe Bot)
**Presentation:** [2. IETF NOC Agent: Meet Joe Bot](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmop-sessb-2-ietf-noc-agent-meet-joe-bot-00)  
**Presenter:** Joe Clarke

*   **Problem Statement:** Rapid deployment requirements in event networks (e.g., IETF NOC) require safe, field-engineer-friendly automation without exposing full administrative credentials or complex interfaces to everyone.
*   **Architecture & Implementation:**
    *   "Joe Bot" is a Slack AI agent front end that communicates with Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers using the Python `FastMCP` framework and Claude as the underlying LLM.
    *   Integrates with internal operational tools: LibreNMS (SNMP monitoring/alerting), NetDisco (topology discovery and reachability troubleshooting), a custom Zero Touch Provisioning (ZTP) tool for switch re-provisioning and image loading, Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) configuration, and OpenTelemetry for observability.
    *   Integrates an external MCP server provided by SFMIX to inspect peering IX port status at 200 Paul.
*   **Design Choices:**
    *   Tools are deliberately outcome/purpose-driven rather than raw generic interfaces (e.g., arbitrary CLI or RESTCONF tools) to prevent unintended side effects.
    *   Destructive actions (e.g., device re-provisioning or clearing rooms) require explicit user confirmation guardrails.
*   **Discussion:**
    *   Qin Wu noted that the NOC Agent resembles a Field Maintenance Engineering (FME) copilot and asked if the agent loop requires a static order of tool execution. Joe Clarke clarified that no fixed tool execution order is enforced; the agent loop dynamically calls tools iteratively based on the query context.
    *   Qin Wu also highlighted related discussions in TM Forum and NMRG regarding knowledge graphs, as well as the AgentOps observability work in OPSAWG.

### NMOP Proposed Rechartering
**Presentation:** [3. NMOP rechartering](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmop-sessb-3-nmop-rechartering-02)  
**Presenter:** Reshad Rahman

*   **Proposed Charter Scope:**
    *   To address working group interest in AI without turning NMOP into a general AI dumping ground, the proposed recharter limits AI scope strictly to conducting practical experiments applied to existing NMOP work: specifically network anomaly detection (`draft-ietf-nmop-network-anomaly-architecture`, `draft-ietf-nmop-network-anomaly-semantics`, `draft-ietf-nmop-network-anomaly-lifecycle`) and incident management.
    *   Explicitly out of scope: Development of foundational AI models, agent architectures, agent protocols, or generic agent frameworks.
    *   Experiment Requirements: Proposals must include defined boundaries, exit criteria, and implementation commitments (e.g., hackathons or open-source software). The WG will limit the number of active concurrent AI experiments initially.
*   **Discussion:**
    *   Rob Bolton requested clarification on whether "agent architectures" includes "agent frameworks". Reshad Rahman and Mahesh Jethanandani confirmed frameworks should also be explicitly excluded to avoid ambiguity.
    *   Dan Voyer asked what specific standardization output is expected from experiments and whether AI NetOps or agent-to-agent JSON modeling fits within the recharter. Reshad Rahman and Benoît Claise emphasized that standardizations (e.g., YANG schema extensions) will depend on experiment outcomes, but generic AI interface work belongs elsewhere.
    *   Dan Bogdanovic and Mike discussed industry adoption speed, arguing that practical AI experiments belong in NMOP to establish operational consensus while the technology is being deployed.
    *   Thomas Graf suggested expanding the anomaly detection charter scope to explicitly cover network post-mortems, and recommended expanding message broker scope to cover IPFIX and BMP to YANG conversions (referencing `draft-ietf-nmop-yang-message-broker-integration` and `draft-ietf-nmop-message-broker-telemetry-message`).

### Experiment Proposal: Incident Server Implementation Using Agentic AI
**Presentation:** [4. Experiment on Incident Server Implementation Using Agentic AI](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmop-sessb-4-experiment-on-incident-server-implementation-using-agentic-ai-00)  
**Presenter:** Henry Yu

*   **Proposal Overview:** Huawei and China Unicom proposed an experiment evaluating AI agents and knowledge graphs inside an Incident Server (aligned with the Incident Server YANG model).
*   **Technical Details:**
    *   Designed an agent cognitive loop for root cause diagnosis.
    *   Uses a 3-layer Knowledge Graph (KG) based on RFC 9940 terminology as a ground truth guardrail to constrain LLM hallucinations.
*   **Potential Standardization Areas:**
    *   Augmenting the Incident YANG model with AI-specific metadata attributes (e.g., root cause confidence scores, token usage metrics during diagnosis).
    *   Standardizing knowledge graph schema representations to support multi-vendor interoperability.
*   **Discussion:**
    *   Joe Clarke questioned why standardizing knowledge graph formats is necessary when LLMs process natural language and Markdown well. Henry Yu and Mike responded that general LLMs lack precise domain context, and Markdown representations do not scale well for complex semantics without causing hallucinations.

### AI-Based Network Management Agent (NMA) and Northbound Task Interface (NTI)
**Presentation:** [5. AI Based Network Management Agent (NMA) and Northbound Task Interface (NTI) Updates and Experiment Proposal](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nmop-sessb-5-ai-based-network-management-agent-nma-and-northbound-task-interface-nti-updates-and-experiment-proposal-00)  
**Presenter:** Paul

*   **Proposal Overview:** Overview of the Network Management Agent (NMA) framework for autonomous task execution (intent handling, planning, diagnosis) and the Northbound Task Interface (NTI) YANG profile. Proposed testing NMA on incident management and anomaly troubleshooting workflows.
*   **Discussion:**
    *   Dan Voyer and Dan Bogdanovic raised questions regarding where NMA/NTI protocol work belongs in the IETF structure and whether YANG task profiles are necessary for agent interaction.
    *   Reshad Rahman and Mahesh Jethanandani clarified that under the proposed recharter, defining NMA architectures or NTI task protocols is not within NMOP scope.
    *   Mahesh Jethanandani suggested that NMA contributors collaborate with Henry Yu's incident server experiment to focus on validating existing WG mechanisms (Incident YANG, YANG Push, NETCONF notifications).

